window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"] = window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"] || {};
window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"][41] = "<p><strong>Where a PM could intervene.</strong> <em>Candidates:</em> a new source for new members. <em>Ranking:</em> what the model predicts (starting a title, finishing it, or still being a member in a month). <em>Re-ranking:</em> a rule to guarantee variety. <em>Presentation:</em> explanations (&quot;Because you watched...&quot;).</p>\n<p><strong>Cold start.</strong> A new member has no history: ask for a few favorite titles at sign-up, use popularity in the country and language, and let the first sessions teach the system; for a new title, use its attributes (genre, cast, language) and a small exposure budget (Worked example 5).</p>\n<p><strong>Failure and degradation.</strong> If the ranking model is slow or down, fall back to a precomputed popular list per country and show it, with no visible error (graceful degradation, C22.7).</p>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> Splitting the problem into stages made the heavy model affordable and gave a PM several places to act, each with a different risk.</p>\n<span id=\"c23-worked-example-3-reading-a-feed-ranking-experiment-with-mixe\"></span>\n<h4>Worked example 3 — Reading a feed ranking experiment with mixed metrics (Illustrative)</h4>\n<p><em>Setting: a social feed of the kind Meta operates. No fact about any real system is claimed.</em></p>\n<p><strong>The situation.</strong> A new feed ranking predicts clicks more accurately than the old one. After a four-week test on 5% of users against a control (invented), the readout is:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Metric</th>\n<th>Result</th>\n<th>95% interval</th>\n<th>Guardrail or goal</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Clicks per user</td>\n<td>+3.1%</td>\n<td>+2.6% to +3.6%</td>\n<td>Goal</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Time spent per user</td>\n<td>+1.2%</td>\n<td>+0.8% to +1.6%</td>\n<td>Goal</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>28-day retention</td>\n<td>−0.15 points</td>\n<td>−0.50 to +0.20</td>\n<td>Margin −0.30</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&quot;I enjoyed my feed&quot; survey score</td>\n<td>−1.0 points</td>\n<td>−1.8 to −0.2</td>\n<td>Margin −0.50</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Reports of unwanted content per 1,000 views</td>\n<td>+4%</td>\n<td>+1% to +7%</td>\n<td>Margin +3%</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Read it in order (FW-C14-07).</strong> <em>Validity:</em> the sample ratio and logging checks pass. <em>Novelty:</em> clicks by week are +4.5%, +3.1%, +2.2% and +1.8%: the effect is still decaying and has not settled, so the +3.1% overall overstates the long-run effect, perhaps by half or more (C14.4). <em>Guardrails:</em> retention's lower bound (−0.50) is outside its margin (−0.30) though the interval includes zero (uncertain, not proven harm); the survey score is clearly lower, with the whole interval below zero and its lower end beyond the margin; reports of unwanted content are up, with the interval partly beyond the margin. <em>Goals:</em> clicks and time are up.</p>\n<p><strong>The diagnosis.</strong> The model improved at the thing it was trained on (clicks) and the system moved toward content that draws clicks and feels worse. Higher engagement together with a lower self-reported enjoyment is the classic pattern of optimizing a proxy (Goodhart, C05).</p>\n<p><strong>Decision (FW-C17-04, FW-C23-07).</strong> Do not launch as is. Investigate which content types and which segments drove the click gain and the survey drop; change the objective, for example by combining the click prediction with a prediction of &quot;valued&quot; (a survey-based or &quot;not interested&quot;-based signal) and a penalty for content that draws reports; and retest for six weeks with a long-term holdout of 1% to check retention. Set the decision rule in advance: launch if the survey score is no worse than −0.2 and retention's lower bound is above −0.30.</p>\n<p><strong>What I'd tell the team.</strong> &quot;The model predicts clicks better. It does not follow that people are better served. We'll keep the gain where people also say they value it.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> A mixed readout is resolved by reading novelty, guardrails and the objective, not by adding up the wins.</p>\n<span id=\"c23-worked-example-4-comparing-search-rankings-precision-and-ndc\"></span>\n<h4>Worked example 4 — Comparing search rankings: precision and NDCG (Illustrative)</h4>\n<p><em>Setting: a web search product of the kind Google offers. No fact about any real system is claimed.</em></p>\n<p><strong>The situation.</strong> Two rankings are compared for one query. Raters judge each of the top five results on a 0-to-3 scale (3 is perfect) (invented). Ranking A returns grades [3, 2, 3, 0, 1] in order; ranking B returns [3, 3, 2, 1, 0].</p>\n<p><strong>Precision at 5.</strong> If a grade of 2 or 3 counts as relevant, A has three relevant results in five (0.6) and B has three (0.6): equal.</p>\n<p><strong>NDCG.</strong> Discounted cumulative gain adds each result's grade divided by log₂(position + 1), so a good result at position 1 counts fully and one at position 5 counts less. A: 3 ÷ log₂2 + 2 ÷ log₂3 + 3 ÷ log₂4 + 0 + 1 ÷ log₂6 = 3 + 1.26 + 1.5 + 0 + 0.39 = 6.15. B: 3 + 3 ÷ 1.585 + 2 ÷ 2 + 1 ÷ 2.32 + 0 = 3 + 1.89 + 1 + 0.43 + 0 = 6.32. The best possible ordering of these grades is B's, so B's normalized score is 1.00 and A's is 6.15 ÷ 6.32 = 0.97. B is better because it puts its second-best result above its weaker ones.</p>\n<p><strong>Why a PM cares.</strong> Two rankings with the same share of relevant results can differ in order, and users look at the top. A measure that ignores position would miss it.</p>\n<p><strong>Is a difference real?</strong> Over a sample of 300 queries, suppose B beats A by 0.03 in average NDCG, with the standard deviation of per-query differences at about 0.15: the standard error is 0.15 ÷ √300 = 0.0087, so the 0.03 difference is about 3.5 standard errors: unlikely to be chance. Also check that raters agree (if two raters disagree on 20% of results, average their grades) and that the gain holds in each language and query type (a gain in English queries may hide a loss in others).</p>\n<p><strong>Add behavior.</strong> Compare click-through, the share of searches with no click, and the share where users reformulate. If NDCG rises and reformulations also rise, investigate before launching.</p>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> Position-aware measures and a significance check turned &quot;looks better&quot; into a defensible comparison.</p>\n<span id=\"c23-worked-example-5-an-exploration-budget-for-new-items-illustr\"></span>\n<h4>Worked example 5 — An exploration budget for new items (Illustrative)</h4>\n<p><em>Setting: an online store of the kind Amazon operates. No fact about any real system is claimed.</em></p>\n<p><strong>The situation.</strong> A recommender trained on past clicks rarely shows new products, because they have no clicks. Sellers add 5,000 new products a day (invented). The store shows about 2,000,000,000 recommendation impressions a day, with an average click rate of 6%.</p>\n<p><strong>How much exposure does a new item need?</strong> To estimate a new item's click rate of about 5% to within a useful margin, 1,000 impressions give a standard error of √(0.05 × 0.95 ÷ 1,000) = 0.0069, so a 95% interval of about ±1.35 points: enough to tell a 5% item from a 2% one, not from a 4% one.</p>\n<p><strong>The budget (FW-C23-06).</strong> 5,000 new items × 1,000 impressions = 5,000,000 impressions a day, which is 5,000,000 ÷ 2,000,000,000 = 0.25% of all impressions.</p>\n<p><strong>The cost.</strong> If new items average a 3% click rate against 6% for established ones, the lost clicks are 5,000,000 × (0.06 − 0.03) = 150,000 a day, which is 150,000 ÷ (2,000,000,000 × 0.06) = 0.125% of all clicks. The price of learning about every new product is about an eighth of one percent of clicks.</p>\n<p><strong>The benefit.</strong> Items that turn out good rise in the ranking; the catalog stays fresh; new sellers have a chance, which keeps supply healthy (a marketplace concern, C19). Without exploration the system would show only items it already knows, and new products would never earn the data to be shown.</p>\n<p><strong>Design details.</strong> Show the exploratory impressions in lower-risk slots and spread them over users; stop showing an item once the estimate is clear (it is good or bad); weight toward items with strong content signals (category, price, images); and watch guardrails (complaints, returns) because new items are riskier. Count exploration impressions separately from normal ones so that evaluation does not confuse them.</p>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> A small, computed budget made exploration affordable and measurable, and showed that the cost of learning is tiny against the cost of a catalog that never changes.</p>\n<span id=\"c23-6-baseline-strong-standout\"></span>\n<h3>6. Baseline, Strong, Standout</h3>\n<p><strong>Prompt:</strong> &quot;Your marketplace's 'recommended for you' module gets many clicks but few purchases. What do you do?&quot;</p>\n<span id=\"c23-baseline-answer\"></span>\n<h4>Baseline answer</h4>\n<p>&quot;I'd train a better model, maybe a deep learning model, on more data so that recommendations are more relevant, and add more features like browsing history.&quot;</p>\n<p><em>What is wrong:</em> a model change with no diagnosis; clicks and purchases are not compared; no mention of what the model predicts or how a fix would be measured.</p>\n<span id=\"c23-strong-answer\"></span>\n<h4>Strong answer</h4>\n<p>&quot;First I'd find out why clicks do not turn into purchases. A likely cause is that the model is trained to predict clicks, so it learns to show items that attract clicks (attractive images, low prices) and not items people want to buy. I'd check what the label is, and break the funnel: click, view, add to cart, purchase, by segment and by position.</p>\n<p>&quot;Options: change the objective to predict purchase or add-to-cart (with fewer labels, so I'd combine signals), filter out items that are out of stock or poor value, and rerank for variety. I'd measure purchase conversion and revenue per session as the primary measures with return rate and satisfaction as guardrails, and test the change against the current module.&quot;</p>\n<span id=\"c23-standout-answer\"></span>\n<h4>Standout answer</h4>\n<p>Everything in the Strong answer, plus:</p>\n<p>&quot;I'd look at where the funnel breaks: if people click and then leave within seconds, the items look good and are not what they want, a mismatch between the label and the goal. I'd check position bias, since top slots draw clicks regardless of fit, by randomizing positions in a small slice to measure how much position alone explains.</p>\n<p>&quot;I'd think about the data: purchases are rarer than clicks, so a purchase-trained model has less data and delayed labels; a combined objective, weighting purchase heavily and click lightly, is a product decision I'd make explicit and revisit. I'd check whether sponsored or high-margin items are being promoted by the ranking, since that would explain clicks without purchases and raise a trust question.</p>\n<p>&quot;For evaluation, an offline gain in predicting purchases is a gate, not a verdict. I'd run an online test for at least two to four weeks to see novelty fade, with a holdout, and guardrails on returns and complaints. I'd check the effect on new items and sellers, because a purchase-only objective can starve new products, so I'd keep a small exploration budget.</p>\n<p>&quot;What would change my plan: if the funnel shows the drop is after the click (price, delivery, stock), the ranking is not the problem and the fix belongs elsewhere.&quot;</p>\n<span id=\"c23-what-moves-an-answer-up-a-band\"></span>\n<h4>What moves an answer up a band</h4>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>From Baseline to Strong</th>\n<th>From Strong to Standout</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Diagnoses with the funnel and questions the label</td>\n<td>Checks position bias, label delay and incentives such as sponsored items</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Proposes an objective change with guardrails</td>\n<td>Uses an offline gate, a multi-week online test and a holdout</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Names primary and guardrail metrics</td>\n<td>Considers new items, sellers and when the ranking is not the cause</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<span id=\"c23-7-company-lens\"></span>\n<h3>7. Company lens</h3>\n<p>All claims describe what companies say publicly or what candidates commonly report, not insider knowledge. Interview formats change <code>[as of 2026-10]</code>.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Company lens: Google [as of 2026-10].</strong> Google publishes guidelines used by human raters to judge search quality, and describes search as understanding queries, retrieving and ranking <code>Public</code> <code>[V: Google Search Quality Evaluator Guidelines]</code>. Candidates commonly report prompts on search relevance, ranking changes and evaluation <code>Inferred</code>. Practical consequence: separate query types and languages, use raters and behavior together, and talk about position-aware measures and trust.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Company lens: Meta [as of 2026-10].</strong> Meta has publicly described changes to feed ranking, including a 2018 shift toward interactions between people, and has published information about how ranking works <code>Public</code> <code>[V: Facebook newsroom, 11 January 2018]</code>. Candidates commonly report prompts on feed ranking, engagement versus wellbeing and ranking experiments <code>Inferred</code>. Practical consequence: separate engagement from satisfaction and long-term effects, read novelty by week, and treat guardrails and creator effects as part of the answer.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Company lens: Microsoft [as of 2026-10].</strong> Microsoft owns a professional network with job and people recommendations and has published responsible-AI principles and standards <code>Public</code> <code>[V: Microsoft's June 2016 announcement of the LinkedIn acquisition; Responsible AI Standard shared publicly in 2022]</code>. Candidates commonly report prompts on recommendations with fairness and enterprise considerations <code>Inferred</code>. Practical consequence: define fairness measures by group, check feedback loops, and involve policy and legal early.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Company lens: Amazon [as of 2026-10].</strong> Amazon's researchers published an influential paper on item-to-item collaborative filtering for recommendations in 2003 <code>Public</code> <code>[V: Linden, Smith and York, IEEE Internet Computing 7(1), 2003]</code>, and the store mixes recommendations with sponsored placements. Candidates commonly report prompts on product recommendations, new-item cold start and seller effects <code>Inferred</code>. Practical consequence: reason about labels (clicks versus purchases), exploration for new products, and trust when paid placements share the page.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Company lens: Apple [as of 2026-10].</strong> Apple describes doing machine learning on the device and using privacy-preserving techniques <code>Public</code> <code>[V: Apple privacy and support pages]</code>. Candidates commonly report prompts on personalization under privacy constraints and on music or media recommendations <code>Inferred</code>. Practical consequence: state what data the personalization needs and where it is processed, and offer controls and explanations.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Company lens: Netflix [as of 2026-10].</strong> Netflix has published papers and blog posts on its recommendation approach, including the point that recommendations drive a large share of viewing and its 2006 prize contest <code>Public</code> <code>[V: Netflix Tech Blog, Beyond the 5 Stars; Netflix Prize 2006]</code>. Candidates commonly report prompts on homepage personalization, evaluation and experimentation <code>Inferred</code>. Practical consequence: judge recommendations on whether people start, finish and keep valuing the service, run long tests, and treat offline accuracy as a gate.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Company lens: Stripe [as of 2026-10].</strong> Stripe offers machine-learning-based fraud screening as a product, and payments companies must balance blocked fraud against blocked good customers <code>Public</code> <code>[V: Stripe pricing page and product descriptions (Radar machine learning)]</code>. Candidates report prompts on risk scoring and thresholds <code>Inferred</code>. Practical consequence: set thresholds by the cost of each error, design review zones, and measure the cost of false declines.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<span id=\"c23-8-common-mistakes\"></span>\n<h3>8. Common mistakes</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>#</th>\n<th>Mistake</th>\n<th>How to detect it</th>\n<th>Fix</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>Model with no decision</td>\n<td>&quot;Use ML to improve it&quot;</td>\n<td>State the decision, label and cost of errors</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>Accuracy for a rare event</td>\n<td>&quot;99% accurate&quot;</td>\n<td>Give base rate, precision and recall</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>One threshold by habit</td>\n<td>A default of 0.5</td>\n<td>Choose by cost and capacity; consider zones</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>A single engagement metric</td>\n<td>Clicks only</td>\n<td>Use the metric stack with long-term and guardrails</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>Offline win as launch case</td>\n<td>&quot;Better AUC&quot;</td>\n<td>Treat as a gate; run an online test</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>Ignoring position bias</td>\n<td>Learning from clicks on the current order</td>\n<td>Randomize a slice; correct for position</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>7</td>\n<td>Ignoring cold start</td>\n<td>New items and users get nothing</td>\n<td>Content signals and an exploration budget</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>8</td>\n<td>Ignoring feedback loops</td>\n<td>The system trains on its own output</td>\n<td>Measure concentration; explore; monitor</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>9</td>\n<td>Reading week one</td>\n<td>A novelty bump taken as the effect</td>\n<td>Read by week; long-term holdout</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>10</td>\n<td>Ignoring creators and groups</td>\n<td>Only average user effects</td>\n<td>Check creator exposure and group-level measures</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<span id=\"c23-9-mental-models-applied\"></span>\n<h3>9. Mental models applied</h3>\n<p>Four models, each tied to a decision in this chapter. <a href=\"#gl-goodharts-law\">Goodhart's law</a> is from C02 and <a href=\"#gl-base-rate\">base rate</a> from C12.</p>\n<p><strong>Goodhart's law</strong> (<code>MM-goodhart</code>, see <a href=\"#gl-goodharts-law\">Goodhart's law</a>, C02). When a measure becomes a target it stops being a good measure. A ranking trained to maximize clicks drifts toward click-attracting content. The defense is a stack of metrics, an objective tied to value on reflection, and guardrails (Worked example 3).</p>\n<p><strong>Explore and exploit</strong> (<code>MM-explore-exploit</code>). A decision-maker must choose between using what it believes is best and trying options to learn; too much exploitation freezes learning and too much exploration wastes value <code>[V: Sutton and Barto, Reinforcement Learning: An Introduction, 2nd edition, MIT Press, 2018, chapter 2 on multi-armed bandits]</code>. In a recommender it is the exploration budget (Worked example 5).</p>\n<p><strong>The Matthew effect</strong> (<code>MM-matthew-effect</code>). Those who have advantage gain more of it (&quot;the rich get richer&quot;) <code>[V: Merton, The Matthew Effect in Science, Science 159(3810), 5 January 1968, pp. 56 to 63]</code>. In ranking it is popularity bias: popular items get exposure, exposure creates popularity. Name it when you see concentration rising.</p>\n<p><strong>Base rates</strong> (<code>MM-base-rate</code>, see <a href=\"#gl-base-rate\">base rate</a>, C12). The rarity of what you are looking for shapes every precision figure. Always ask how common the thing is before judging a classifier (Worked example 1).</p>\n<span id=\"c23-engineering-design-and-data-science-perspectives\"></span>\n<h3>Engineering, design and data-science perspectives</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Engineering and ML engineering.</strong> They build and monitor the model and the serving system. Ask what the model predicts, what data it uses and how fresh, how it is evaluated offline, what the latency and cost are, and what the fallback is when it fails. Agree the logging you need for counterfactual evaluation before launch.</li>\n<li><strong>Design and research.</strong> Designers decide how recommendations are explained and how users give feedback (&quot;not interested,&quot; controls to reset). Research shows what people actually value, which is the source of better labels and survey measures.</li>\n<li><strong>Data science.</strong> They own metric definitions, experiments and the diagnosis of changes. Ask for effects by week, by segment and by creator side, for the long-term holdout, and for how interference is handled.</li>\n<li><strong>Policy, legal, trust and safety.</strong> They define harms, fairness requirements and what content may be promoted. Bring them in when a ranking change touches sensitive content, groups of people or creators' livelihoods.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"c23-checklist-before-you-propose-a-change-to-a-ranked-system\"></span>\n<h3>Checklist: before you propose a change to a ranked system</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Have I said what the model predicts, from what label, and whether that matches the goal?</li>\n<li>Do I know the base rate, the cost of each error and the review capacity before choosing a threshold?</li>\n<li>Can I describe the system by stage and say where the problem is?</li>\n<li>Do I have a metric stack with satisfaction, long-term and guardrail measures, not just clicks?</li>\n<li>Have I named data problems (position bias, label noise, selection bias, cold start)?</li>\n<li>Have I considered feedback loops, popularity bias and creator incentives, and an exploration budget?</li>\n<li>Is the experiment plan written (offline gate, novelty by week, interference, long-term holdout, decision rule)?</li>\n<li>Have I said what would change my recommendation?</li>\n</ol>\n<span id=\"c23-10-case-studies\"></span>\n<h3>10. Case studies</h3>\n<span id=\"cs-c23-01\"></span>\n<h5>CS-C23-01 · The music app that learned from its own top 50 · Illustrative · Beginner</h5>\n<p><em>Fictional composite. Every number is invented (invented).</em></p>\n<p><strong>Situation.</strong> A music app with 8,000,000 tracks recommends songs on its home screen. The recommender is trained on listening data: songs that were played are positive examples. Six months after a retrain, product managers notice that the top 50 songs account for 62% of all plays, up from 40% when the system launched, and that new releases get only 1.5% of home-screen impressions. Independent artists complain that no one hears their songs.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> A model trained on the app's own plays, a home screen with limited slots, and a metric (plays per user) that is rising.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> Plays per user are up 4% over six months. The share of listeners who play at least one song they had never heard before in a week has fallen from 31% to 22%. Thirty-day retention is flat.</p>\n<p><strong>Problem definition.</strong> As an observable problem: &quot;The system recommends what is already popular, so popular songs gain plays and new songs get none, and plays per user rise while discovery falls.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Analysis.</strong> A feedback loop (FW-C23-06): what is shown gets played, plays train the model, and the model shows more of the same. Popularity bias concentrates exposure; new songs have no plays, so they are never shown (cold start). The plays metric hides this because familiar songs are easy to play. Exploring has a cost: songs the system is unsure of are skipped more often. Suppose an exploratory slot is skipped 55% of the time and a normal slot 25% of the time. If 8% of slots are exploratory, the overall skip rate rises by 0.08 × (0.55 − 0.25) = 2.4 points.</p>\n<p><strong>Options.</strong></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Option</th>\n<th>Optimizes</th>\n<th>Costs</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>A. Leave it; plays are rising</td>\n<td>Short-term plays</td>\n<td>Discovery keeps falling; artists leave; long-term risk</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B. Reserve 8% of home-screen slots for exploration, with new releases and unfamiliar songs matched to taste</td>\n<td>Discovery and fresh data</td>\n<td>A higher skip rate on those slots (about 2.4 points overall)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>C. Remove popularity from the model</td>\n<td>A flatter distribution</td>\n<td>Worse recommendations for most listeners</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> B costs a small, measurable drop in immediate smoothness and buys learning and discovery. A is easy and reinforces the loop. C swings too far.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision.</strong> Choose B, with exploration slots chosen by content similarity to taste, and a guardrail on skip rate and session length. Measure the top-50 share, the share of listeners who discover a new song in a week, new-release impressions, retention at 90 days and the skip rate. Run it as an experiment with a holdout for eight weeks.</p>\n<p><strong>Outcome.</strong> In this fictional scenario, after eight weeks the top-50 share fell from 62% to 55%, new-release impressions rose from 1.5% to 6%, the share of listeners discovering a new song in a week rose from 22% to 29%, the overall skip rate rose 2.3 points, and 90-day retention was 0.8 points higher than the holdout (an interval of +0.2 to +1.4). The team kept the budget and added it to its dashboards.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative outcomes.</strong> If retention had not moved, discovery would still be valuable for the artists' side of the platform, and the team would weigh the skip cost against it. If the skip rate had risen by 6 points, the exploratory slots would have been too poorly matched and the team would have tuned the candidate source. The lesson: a metric that rewards the familiar will build a system that only shows the familiar; measure discovery too.</p>\n<span id=\"cs-c23-02\"></span>\n<h5>CS-C23-02 · The Netflix Prize, 2006 to 2009: an accuracy contest and what shipped · Verified · Advanced</h5>\n<p><em>Sources (types): Netflix's own engineering account, &quot;Netflix Recommendations: Beyond the 5 stars (Part 1)&quot; by Xavier Amatriain and Justin Basilico (Netflix Tech Blog, 6 April 2012), read in full, a primary source for what Netflix did with the contest's results; the Associated Press report of the award (21 September 2009), for the winners and the number of contestants; the contest's results as summarized in reference coverage (Wikipedia) and a statistics paper on the contest (Feuerverger, He and Khatri, Statistical Science, 2012), for the data set and the number of contestants. The analysis is the author's. The sources do not reveal Netflix's internal evaluation beyond what its post says. Re-read in October 2026.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Situation.</strong> In October 2006 Netflix launched a public contest, the Netflix Prize, offering $1 million to the first team to improve the accuracy of its movie recommendation system, Cinematch, by 10%. Netflix said it needed a proxy question that was easier to evaluate: the root mean squared error (RMSE) of predicted star ratings, where Cinematch scored 0.9525 on the test set and the winning score had to be 0.8572 or lower. By the end the Associated Press reported more than 51,000 contestants from 186 countries <code>[V: AP, 21 September 2009]</code>. A year into the contest the first Progress Prize went to the team Korbell (BellKor), whose solution combined 107 algorithms, after more than 2,000 hours of work by the team's own report, for an 8.43% improvement, and the team gave Netflix its source code <code>[V: Netflix Tech Blog, 2012]</code>. Netflix said that the two best-performing algorithms in that ensemble, a matrix-factorization method (0.8914 RMSE on its own) and a restricted Boltzmann machine (0.8990 on its own; a linear blend of the two gave 0.88), were put into production and were still in use in April 2012, after work to overcome limitations: they were built for the 100 million ratings of the contest data, not the more than 5 billion Netflix had, and were not built to adapt as members added ratings. On 21 September 2009 the grand prize was awarded in New York to BellKor's Pragmatic Chaos, a seven-member team formed by merging earlier teams, for an improvement reported as 10.06% <code>[V: AP; reference summary]</code>; on the public leaderboard it and a rival team, The Ensemble, stood at about 10.09% and 10.10%, and BellKor's Pragmatic Chaos won by submitting its final entry minutes earlier <code>[V: AP]</code>. Netflix described the grand-prize ensemble as blending hundreds of predictive models, said that it evaluated some of the new methods offline but that the additional accuracy gains it measured did not seem to justify the engineering effort needed to bring them into production, and noted that its focus had shifted and that its business had moved toward streaming (launched in 2007), which changed how data was collected and recommendations were delivered <code>[V: Netflix Tech Blog, 2012]</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> A contest dataset that was a small, frozen sample of ratings; a production system with orders of magnitude more data that changed continuously; a proxy metric (error in predicted ratings) standing in for the product's actual goal; and a business changing from mailing discs to streaming.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> As reported: the dates; the prize and target; the proxy metric; the baseline and target RMSE; the number of participants; the 2007 and 2009 results; Netflix's statement about what was and was not deployed and why. Contest scores were computed on a public &quot;quiz&quot; subset, where Cinematch scored 0.9514, and on a hidden test set, where it scored 0.9525; Netflix's post quotes the test-set figures <code>[V: Netflix post; reference summary]</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>Problem definition.</strong> <em>The author's reading.</em> The company wanted better recommendations, and it measured an easier thing: accuracy at predicting ratings. As an observable problem: &quot;An improvement on the proxy metric was large and did not translate in full to the product.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Analysis.</strong> <em>The author's analysis, through this chapter's tools.</em></p>\n<p><em>The proxy.</em> Predicting the star rating a member would give a movie is not the same as choosing which titles to put in front of them. Ratings were explicit and sparse; the product's key decisions concern ranking a few titles out of thousands, where the top of the list matters most (C23.5's lesson on position-aware measures). A small gain in rating error need not change what appears on the screen. This is the proxy-worship risk of C23.1, and Netflix itself, by its account, used the contest to find methods and then evaluated them against the production problem.</p>\n<p><em>Offline against online (C23.9).</em> The contest was an offline evaluation on a fixed dataset. Netflix's reported decision to adopt a blend of two methods and not the winning ensemble shows offline results used as a gate: the cost of building and maintaining a 100-algorithm ensemble was weighed against measured gains.</p>\n<p><em>Scale and adaptivity.</em> Methods developed for 100 million ratings had to be reworked for 5 billion and for members who add ratings continuously: engineering constraints that a contest does not test (C21, C22).</p>\n<p><em>The data changed.</em> A move to streaming replaced explicit ratings with implicit signals (what members play, how long, when they stop), which are more plentiful and noisier, and shifted the problem from predicting ratings to ranking what to play next. The question the contest answered was becoming less central.</p>\n<p><em>What the evidence does not show.</em> Netflix's internal measurements of the gain from each method, the exact production blend's effect on retention, and how its metrics evolved. The case supports an analysis of proxies and offline evaluation, not a judgment about the contest's cost or value.</p>\n<p><strong>Options.</strong> <em>The author's reconstruction of choices a team could face after the contest; not a record of Netflix's deliberations.</em></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Option</th>\n<th>Optimizes</th>\n<th>Costs</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>A. Deploy the full grand-prize ensemble</td>\n<td>The contest's measured accuracy</td>\n<td>Large engineering and maintenance cost; a gain on a proxy</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B. Deploy the simpler components that gave most of the gain, adapted to production data</td>\n<td>Value per engineering effort</td>\n<td>Gives up the last increments of accuracy</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>C. Redefine the objective around streaming behavior and ranking, and invest there</td>\n<td>Fit with the product</td>\n<td>Needs new data, metrics and experiments</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> A buys accuracy at a high cost and on a proxy. B captures most of the gain more cheaply. C is the most relevant and the most work, and is where a business that has changed its product must go.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision.</strong> As reported, Netflix deployed a blend of two methods from the 2007 solution, did not deploy the grand-prize ensemble, and shifted its recommendation work toward the streaming product.</p>\n<p><strong>Outcome.</strong> Reported: the winning score, the partial deployment, the stated reason, and, in Netflix's later publications, an emphasis on ranking and on testing with members. Financial results of the contest are outside the sources used here.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative outcomes.</strong> <em>Inference.</em> Had the ensemble been deployed, the maintenance burden might have slowed later work for little product gain; had no contest been held, the company would not have had the matrix-factorization methods that became standard. A reader should avoid hindsight: a public contest was a cheap way to find methods. The method to carry away: know what your metric stands in for, treat offline gains as a gate with a price on complexity, test on the production problem with real members, and expect the product's data and objective to change.</p>\n<span id=\"c23-11-interview-questions-and-model-answers\"></span>\n<h3>11. Interview questions and model answers</h3>\n<p>Ten questions. Model answers are <code>Illustrative</code> (composite candidates), every figure is an assumption, and the arithmetic was computed. Each ends with follow-ups and answers. Where a real company's product is the setting, the answer reasons from public information and states no internal fact. The answers are fuller than a first pass in a real round.</p>\n<span id=\"iq-c23-01\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C23-01 · Beginner · ai · AI</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> Explain in plain terms how a recommendation system decides what to show you.</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;It uses an algorithm that learns what you like.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Describes candidate generation, ranking and re-ranking, and what data it learns from.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Explains why stages exist, what the model predicts and how that choice shapes results, mentions cold start and feedback loops, and keeps it plain.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> Think of a very large store and a very small shop window. The system has millions of items and room to show twenty, so it works in steps.</p>\n<p>First it narrows the store to a few hundred plausible items, cheaply: things similar to what you liked, things from people you follow, what is popular where you are, what is new in your favorite topics. This step is about not missing good things.</p>\n<p>Then a more careful model scores each of those few hundred and puts them in order. The model has learned from past behavior: what people like you watched, finished, liked or skipped. What it predicts matters: if it predicts clicks, the order favors things that attract clicks; if it predicts &quot;watched most of it and did not regret it,&quot; the order favors something else.</p>\n<p>Then rules adjust the list: do not show ten items from one creator, remove anything unsafe or already seen, give new items a small chance. Finally the screen decides how to present them.</p>\n<p>Two things make it hard. A new user or new item has no history (the cold start), so the system leans on item attributes and popularity until it learns. And the system learns from what it showed, so it can keep showing more of the same: a feedback loop that product teams must watch.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> Why not score every item for every user? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;The cost. Scoring millions of items per request with a heavy model would need far more computers and time than the user will wait, a few hundred milliseconds. The staged design keeps the heavy model for a few hundred candidates and spends far less.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What does &quot;the model predicts clicks&quot; imply for the product? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;It will tend to promote whatever draws clicks, including sensational or misleading items, because that is what it is rewarded for. A product team can change the target (for example, include completion or satisfaction signals) and should check that what the model optimizes is what people value.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c23-02\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C23-02 · Beginner · ai · AI</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> A fraud model is 99% accurate. Is that good?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Yes, 99% is excellent.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Asks the base rate and uses precision and recall.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Shows with numbers that a useless model can match the accuracy, relates it to cost and thresholds, and says what to ask for instead.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> Not necessarily; accuracy alone says little when the thing you are looking for is rare. Suppose 1% of 1,000,000 transactions are fraud: 10,000 fraudulent and 990,000 legitimate. A model that labels everything legitimate is right 990,000 times: 99% accurate, and catches no fraud at all (recall 0%).</p>\n<p>Now take a useful model that catches 8,000 of the 10,000 frauds (recall 80%) and wrongly flags 8,000 legitimate transactions. Its correct decisions are the 8,000 frauds caught plus the 982,000 legitimate transactions it left alone: 990,000, so it is also 99.0% accurate. The same accuracy describes a model that does nothing and a model that catches 80% of fraud. That is why accuracy is the wrong measure here.</p>\n<p>What I would ask for instead: the base rate; precision (of what we flag, how much is fraud: here 50%) and recall (of all fraud, how much we catch: 80%); the cost of each error (a blocked good customer against a missed fraud); and performance at the threshold we plan to use, on recent data and by segment. Then I would decide whether the model is good by whether its total cost is lower than the alternatives within our review capacity.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What if the base rate drops to 0.1%? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Precision falls sharply even if the model is unchanged: with the same 80% recall and about a 0.8% false-positive rate, it catches 800 frauds and flags about 8,000 good transactions, a precision of about 9%. The queue becomes mostly good transactions, so cost per fraud caught rises about tenfold, and the threshold should be revisited.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> Why not just report recall? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Recall alone rewards flagging everything, which has perfect recall and useless precision. The pair, with the cost of each error, shows the tradeoff.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c23-03\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C23-03 · Intermediate · ai · AI · Apple</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> Improve the recommendations in a music app for a user who plays the same ten songs.</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Recommend more songs that are similar to those ten.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Recognizes that familiarity is rewarded and proposes some exploration and a way to measure it.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Diagnoses whether the user wants novelty, designs a controlled exploration slot, defines satisfaction and long-term measures, considers privacy and controls, and plans a test.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> The setting is general; I know such products only from public information, and nothing here describes any real design. The first question is whether this is a problem. A person who plays the same ten songs may be perfectly satisfied (a workout list, a comfort album). The aim is to help the people who would like more, without annoying those who would not.</p>\n<p><em>Diagnose.</em> Which of these users are stuck (they have skipped recent suggestions, searched for new music elsewhere, or their listening has narrowed over months) and which are content? A recommender trained on plays will favor the familiar songs because plays are what it was rewarded for; that is a feedback loop.</p>\n<p><em>Options.</em> (1) <em>Blend:</em> keep the familiar songs and add adjacent ones (same mood, tempo or artist's other work), a small step from taste, in a clearly labeled &quot;something new&quot; slot. (2) <em>An exploration budget:</em> a small share of slots, say 8%, chosen to learn. (3) <em>User control:</em> a &quot;more like this / less like this&quot; signal and a reset, which also respects preference. (4) <em>A separate discovery surface</em> so that the main screen can stay familiar for those who want that.</p>\n<p><em>Cost.</em> Exploratory slots are accepted less often than familiar ones. Suppose (invented) a new song is played through 30% of the time against 85% for a familiar one: 8 exploratory slots per 100 lose about (0.85 − 0.30) × 8 = 4.4 completed plays per 100 slots in the short term, and yield about 2.4 accepted new songs. The product must decide that the discovery is worth the short-term cost for users who show a desire for it.</p>\n<p><em>Metrics.</em> Primary: new songs added to a library or played to completion at least twice (a satisfaction signal). Guardrails: overall skip rate and session length, &quot;not interested&quot; feedback. Long term: 90-day retention against a holdout. Segment by user type, since the effect differs.</p>\n<p><em>Privacy.</em> Personalization uses listening history; state what is used and where it is processed, and let users clear it.</p>\n<p><em>Test.</em> A multi-week test with a holdout, reading effects by week for novelty, and by user segment.</p>\n<p><em>Company note.</em> Apple is the setting only: <code>Inferred</code> from candidate reports of media-recommendation prompts <code>[unverified: candidate-reported]</code>. No claim is made about any real product.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How do you know whether a user wants novelty? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Behavior: skipping the same songs, searching for songs outside the ten, narrowing listening over time compared with their own history, and explicit signals (a 'show me something new' tap). I'd start with those users, and offer others a control and not a change.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What if the exploration slots lower the skip-rate guardrail? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Make exploration better matched: choose adjacent songs, place them after a familiar one, and reduce the share until the guardrail holds. If no setting gives a net benefit for a segment, leave that segment on the familiar experience.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c23-04\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C23-04 · Intermediate · ai · AI · Netflix</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> How would you measure whether homepage personalization is working, beyond clicks?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Click-through rate on recommended titles.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Uses plays and completion and retention.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Explains why clicks mislead, defines qualified plays, a layered stack with short and long term and diversity, plans holdouts, and notes novelty and offline gates.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> The setting is general; nothing here describes any real measurement. Clicks measure curiosity about a thumbnail, not whether the title was worth watching. A better artwork or a catchy title can raise clicks and lower satisfaction.</p>\n<p><em>Suppose</em> (invented) a change raises clicks on recommended titles by 5%, and 40% of the plays it adds are abandoned within two minutes. The extra plays are worth little. So I'd define a <em>qualified play</em>: a play that reaches a set point, for example 70% of an episode or a second episode. If qualified plays rose only 0.5%, the real gain is a tenth of the click gain.</p>\n<p><em>The stack (FW-C23-05).</em> Immediate: play starts and the time to first play (how quickly a member finds something). Satisfaction: qualified plays, completions, ratings or thumbs where given, and the share of members who say they found something to watch. Long term: retention and return frequency at 28 and 90 days, which is where the value of recommendations shows. Diversity and discovery: the variety of titles and genres watched, the share of plays of titles the member has not seen the like of before, and exposure for newer titles. Guardrails: search use (a sign that recommendations failed), complaints, cost.</p>\n<p><em>Experiments.</em> Offline accuracy is a gate; the judge is an online test with a holdout, run for weeks to let novelty fade, with a long-term holdout for retention. Analyze by member segment (new members, heavy viewers, kids' profiles).</p>\n<p><em>What I'd watch for.</em> A rise in clicks with a rise in search (members are not finding what they want on the homepage), and concentration (most plays from a few titles).</p>\n<p><em>Company note.</em> Netflix is the setting only: <code>Inferred</code> from candidate reports of personalization and measurement prompts <code>[unverified: candidate-reported]</code>. No Netflix data or practice is claimed.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> Why is retention the right long-term measure? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;A subscription's value depends on members continuing to find enough they enjoy; recommendations are meant to make that easier. If a change raised plays and members still left at the same rate, the system was not helping them. Retention is slow and noisy, so use a long holdout and combine it with earlier satisfaction signals.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How do you avoid optimizing for easy, short content? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Measure satisfaction per qualified play and long-term retention, not total plays, and check the mix of what is watched. If the model's predicted 'finish' favors short items, add a measure of how much members valued what they finished.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c23-05\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C23-05 · Intermediate · ai · AI · Google</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> Search results got worse for a class of queries. How do you find out why and what to do?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Retrain the ranking model.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Segments by query type and checks stages.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Uses raters and behavior together, shows how an overall average can hide a class problem, locates the stage, handles language and freshness, and plans a fix with a test and monitoring.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> The setting is general; nothing here describes any real system. First, confirm and size the problem: which queries, how much worse, since when.</p>\n<p><em>Why the average hides it.</em> Suppose (invented) a class, say recipe queries, is 6% of traffic, and precision at 5 for the class fell from 0.72 to 0.64, a drop of 0.08. The overall figure moves by 0.06 × 0.08 = 0.0048, from 0.800 to 0.795: invisible in the average. So I segment by query type, topic, language and device before I trust any overall number.</p>\n<p><em>Diagnose by stage (FW-C23-04).</em> <em>Understanding:</em> is the system misreading these queries (spelling, synonyms, a language or script issue)? <em>Retrieval:</em> are good results absent from the candidates (an index or freshness problem)? <em>Ranking:</em> are good results retrieved but ranked low (a new signal or a model change)? <em>Presentation:</em> did a layout change bury results? Compare before and after on a rated sample, and look at what changed on the date the quality fell (a ranking update, an index change, a new data source).</p>\n<p><em>Use two kinds of evidence.</em> Human raters judging results on a 0-to-3 scale with NDCG (position-aware) show quality; behavior (clicks, reformulations, abandonment) shows intent. If raters say results are worse and reformulations rose, the problem is real. If only clicks fell, a layout change may explain it.</p>\n<p><em>Fix.</em> Depending on the stage: repair the index or freshness; restore a signal that the update dropped, or add a feature for the class; adjust the ranking for that class; then test. Roll back the change if it clearly caused the drop (C17).</p>\n<p><em>Monitor.</em> Add the class to the standing quality dashboard so that the next regression is seen early, and add raters' test queries for it.</p>\n<p><em>Company note.</em> Google is the setting only: <code>Inferred</code> from candidate reports of search quality prompts <code>[unverified: candidate-reported]</code>. No Google data or practice is claimed.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How do you tell a ranking problem from a retrieval problem? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Look for a known good result for a sample of the failing queries: if it is not in the candidate set, retrieval or the index is at fault; if it is retrieved but ranked low, ranking is at fault. Raters or a hand-picked list of good results make the test concrete.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What if raters and clicks disagree? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Investigate before acting: raters may misjudge intent, and clicks are biased by position and layout. Look at a few disagreements in detail, check whether the layout changed, and prefer a controlled experiment to settle it.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c23-06\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C23-06 · Hard · ai · AI · Meta</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> A new feed ranking raises time spent by 5%, but users say they feel worse. What do you do?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Ship it; time spent is up,&quot; or &quot;kill it.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Treats the survey as a guardrail and investigates the cause.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Distinguishes valued from unvalued time, finds which content and segments drive it, changes the objective, reads novelty and retention, plans a longer test with a decision rule, and weighs creators and harm.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> The setting is general; nothing here describes any real system. Time spent is an engagement measure; how people feel is a satisfaction and wellbeing measure. When they diverge, I do not average them; I find out why, because the system may be succeeding at the wrong thing (Goodhart).</p>\n<p><em>Investigate.</em> (1) <em>Composition:</em> which content types, creators and formats account for the added time, and what do people say about those types? (2) <em>Segments:</em> who feels worse (teens, heavy users, new users)? Averages can hide a group that is harmed. (3) <em>Time shape:</em> is the +5% a novelty bump? Read effects by week; suppose weekly gains are +7%, +5%, +4% and +3.5%: still falling, so the settled effect is smaller. (4) <em>Long term:</em> retention and return frequency at 28 days against a holdout. (5) <em>Other guardrails:</em> reports of unwanted content, &quot;not interested&quot; taps, complaints.</p>\n<p><em>Valued time.</em> Suppose (invented) the survey shows a 1.5-point drop in &quot;I enjoyed my feed&quot; and 60% of the extra time comes from content types with the lowest enjoyment. Then &quot;valued time&quot; (time weighted by a satisfaction signal) may be flat or down even though raw time is up.</p>\n<p><em>Options.</em> (A) Ship, accepting the cost. (B) Change the objective: combine the engagement prediction with a prediction of &quot;valued&quot; (from surveys or &quot;not interested&quot; signals) and a penalty for low-quality content, then retest. (C) Ship only to segments where satisfaction holds. (D) Roll back.</p>\n<p><em>Decision.</em> B, with a six-week test and a long-term holdout, and a rule written in advance: launch if the survey score is no worse than −0.3, retention's lower bound is above the margin and reports do not rise beyond +3%. Do not ship the +5% while the survey and the novelty read are open.</p>\n<p><em>Creators and harm.</em> A ranking that favors certain formats changes what creators make; check exposure for different creators, and escalate to policy and safety if the content type is harmful.</p>\n<p><em>Company note.</em> Meta is the setting only: <code>Inferred</code> from candidate reports of feed and engagement-versus-wellbeing prompts <code>[unverified: candidate-reported]</code>. No Meta data or practice is claimed.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How can survey results be trusted if only a few respond? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Treat them as a signal with an interval and bias in mind: sample randomly within the test, ask the same short question to both arms, check response rates by arm and by segment, and corroborate with behavior (not-interested taps, hiding posts, returning less). A drop that shows in several independent measures is credible.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What if leadership wants the time-spent gain for this quarter? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Show the cost in their terms: the novelty decay, the retention risk and the share of the gain that is unvalued time. Offer the version that keeps most of the gain with the guardrails satisfied (a changed objective), and a date for the decision. If they still decide to ship, ship to a limited share and keep the holdout so that the effect is measured.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c23-07\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C23-07 · Hard · ai · AI · Amazon</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> How would you improve recommendations for new products with no history, when paid placements share the page?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Recommend popular products.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Uses content-based signals and exploration for cold start.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Sizes the exploration budget, separates organic from sponsored placement, designs evaluation and guardrails, addresses seller fairness and trust, and avoids feedback loops.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> The setting is general; nothing here describes any real system. A recommender trained on purchases and clicks rarely shows new products, because they have no data; so they never get data.</p>\n<p><em>Use what a new product does have.</em> Its attributes (category, brand, price, images, description), the seller's history, and similarity to products shoppers already like. These content signals let the ranking guess before behavior exists.</p>\n<p><em>Add an exploration budget.</em> Show each new product to enough shoppers to estimate its click rate: about 1,000 impressions give a 95% interval of about ±1.35 points at a 5% rate. With 5,000 new products a day that is 5,000,000 impressions, which is 0.25% of 2 billion daily impressions. If new products click at 3% against 6% for established ones, the cost is about 150,000 clicks a day, 0.125% of all clicks. Cheap relative to a catalog that never changes. Spread the exposure across shoppers and slots, stop showing an item once the estimate is clear, and weight toward items with strong content signals.</p>\n<p><em>Sponsored placements.</em> Paid placements mean a seller can buy exposure. Keep organic recommendations and paid placements distinct and clearly labeled, and make sure that the organic model is not trained on clicks that were driven by paid position (position bias and exposure bias). Otherwise paid products will look better than they are and the system will learn to favor them.</p>\n<p><em>Evaluation.</em> Online test with a holdout; measure purchases and revenue per session, return rates and satisfaction, new-product success (the share that reach a minimum sales level), and seller diversity. Guardrails: complaints, returns, and the share of the page that is paid.</p>\n<p><em>Fairness and trust.</em> New sellers need a chance; shoppers need to trust that recommendations are not only the highest bidder's. Publish clear rules and monitor concentration.</p>\n<p><em>Company note.</em> Amazon is the setting only: <code>Inferred</code> from candidate reports of recommendation and marketplace prompts <code>[unverified: candidate-reported]</code>. No Amazon data or practice is claimed.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How do you keep the exploration budget from becoming an advertising loophole? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Keep it algorithmic and separate from paid placement: eligibility by content quality signals, a cap per seller, and no payment influence. Audit who receives exploration exposure, so that the budget serves learning and not whoever pays.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What if new products have high return rates? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Treat returns as a guardrail and a label: reduce exposure for items with high returns after a minimum number of sales, and use early return signals in ranking, since a click or a sale that is returned is not a success.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c23-08\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C23-08 · Hard · ai · AI · Microsoft</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> Design the fairness checks for a system that recommends jobs to people.</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Remove gender and race from the data.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Measures outcomes by group and considers proxies.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Defines exposure and selection measures, tests significance, considers feedback loops and the difference between measuring and deciding, involves legal and policy, and sets monitoring and remedies.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> The setting is general; nothing here describes any real system. Removing sensitive attributes from the data does not remove bias, because other features (location, schools, past job titles, even names or the time of activity) can stand in for them, and because the data itself reflects past outcomes. So the check must measure outcomes.</p>\n<p><em>What to measure.</em> (1) <em>Exposure:</em> how often qualified people in each group are shown a given type of job, especially higher-paying ones. (2) <em>Outcomes:</em> application and response rates by group, for equally qualified users. (3) <em>Error rates:</em> whether the model's mistakes (qualified people not shown a job) are larger for some groups. Group definitions need care and legal guidance, and in many places the system may not hold the sensitive attribute, so audits may use opt-in data or a trusted third party.</p>\n<p><em>An example check.</em> Suppose among 10,000 equally qualified users in each of two groups, a job type is shown to 30% of group A and 21% of group B. The ratio of rates is 0.21 ÷ 0.30 = 0.70. A commonly used rule of thumb in United States employment settings treats a ratio below 0.8 as a sign of possible adverse impact <code>[V: Uniform Guidelines on Employee Selection Procedures (1978), 29 CFR 1607: the four-fifths rule is a rule of thumb and not determinative]</code>. The standard error of the difference is about 0.6 points, so the 9-point gap is about 15 standard errors: not chance. That triggers an investigation, not an automatic verdict.</p>\n<p><em>Find the cause.</em> Is it the candidate stage (the job never enters the candidate set for group B), the ranking (features that proxy for group), the data (past hires), or the interface? Check the feedback loop: if users click what they are shown and the model learns from clicks, an early gap can grow.</p>\n<p><em>Remedies.</em> Re-examine features that act as proxies; balance exposure by adding candidates or re-ranking to meet an agreed floor; improve labels (use qualification signals, not past hiring alone); give users controls and transparency about why a job is shown.</p>\n<p><em>Governance.</em> Fairness definitions can conflict (equal exposure against equal accuracy), and choosing among them is a policy decision with legal, ethical and business input, not an engineering default. Document the choice, the tests, the thresholds for action, and the monitoring cadence.</p>\n<p><em>Company note.</em> Microsoft is the setting only: <code>Inferred</code> from candidate reports of recommendation and responsible-AI prompts <code>[unverified: candidate-reported]</code>. No Microsoft data or practice is claimed; the 0.8 ratio is a widely cited rule of thumb and the law varies by jurisdiction.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> If you cannot collect the sensitive attribute, how do you check? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Use a voluntary, consented survey of a sample to get group labels for auditing only, use a trusted third party, or test with synthetic matched profiles that differ only in a proxy (a name or a school). Each has limits, which should be stated; none replaces monitoring outcomes.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What if equalizing exposure lowers relevance? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Measure the cost. Often the loss is small; if it is large, ask whether the model's relevance estimate itself reflects the bias. The tradeoff is a decision for the company with policy and legal input, with the numbers shown.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c23-09\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C23-09 · Hard · ai · AI · Stripe</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> Design the decisioning for a payments fraud score: what happens at each score range?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Block payments with a high fraud score.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Uses zones (allow, review, block) with costs.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Computes cost by zone, finds the review break-even, measures false declines, considers delayed labels, drift and feedback, and sets monitoring and rules around the model.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> The setting is general; nothing here describes any real system. A single block-or-allow threshold wastes the middle: payments that are risky but not clearly fraud. I'd use three zones.</p>\n<p><em>Numbers (invented).</em> 10,000,000 payments a day, 0.5% fraudulent: 50,000. Fraud costs $95 per fraudulent payment (the loss plus fees). A human review costs $1 and catches 80% of the fraud in the review zone. Blocking a legitimate payment costs $5 of margin plus some customer goodwill.</p>\n<p><em>Zones.</em> <em>Block</em> (very high scores): 20,000 payments, 18,000 fraudulent, so 90% precision; 2,000 good payments are blocked. <em>Review</em> (middle): 150,000 payments with 25,000 fraudulent (16.7%); reviewers catch 20,000 and miss 5,000. <em>Allow</em> (low scores): 9,830,000 payments with 7,000 fraudulent.</p>\n<p><em>Cost.</em> Fraud that gets through: (7,000 + 5,000) × $95 = $1,140,000. Review: 150,000 × $1 = $150,000. Blocked good payments: 2,000 × $5 = $10,000. Total about $1.30 million a day, against $4.75 million with no screening (50,000 × $95).</p>\n<p><em>Where to put the review boundary.</em> Moving payments into review pays if the fraud rate in the added band exceeds the break-even: cost of review per payment ÷ (catch rate × fraud cost) = $1 ÷ (0.8 × $95) = 1.3%. If a band of 100,000 payments just below the review zone has a 3% fraud rate (3,000 frauds), reviewing it costs $100,000 and prevents 0.8 × 3,000 × $95 = $228,000: a net gain of $128,000 a day. So extend review down to the point where the fraud rate falls toward 1.3%.</p>\n<p><em>Beyond the score.</em> Rules for known patterns (a card tested with many tiny charges), step-up verification for risky payments (ask for an extra check before blocking), and different thresholds by merchant type and amount. Measure the <em>false decline</em> rate and its effect on good customers and merchants, not just fraud caught.</p>\n<p><em>Data realities.</em> Labels arrive late (disputes come weeks later) and only for payments that were allowed; payments that were blocked never reveal whether they were fraud. That selection bias means the model's training data excludes what it blocked; keep a small random allowance or use review outcomes to learn. Fraudsters adapt, so the model drifts: monitor score distributions and fraud rates, retrain often, and keep a human-in-the-loop for new patterns.</p>\n<p><em>Company note.</em> Stripe is the setting only: <code>Public</code> that Stripe offers machine-learning-based fraud screening, <code>Inferred</code> from candidate reports of risk-scoring prompts <code>[V: Stripe Radar machine-learning fraud screening; the prompt descriptions are candidate-reported and unverified]</code>. No claim is made about any real system's numbers or design.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How do you learn about fraud in payments you blocked? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;You mostly cannot, so the blocked set is a blind spot. Allow a small random sample of high-score payments through under strict limits to measure true fraud, use review outcomes, and compare against chargebacks from similar allowed payments. The sample costs a small amount of fraud and keeps the model honest.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> A merchant complains that too many good customers are declined. What do you do? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Look at that merchant's decline rate and its review outcomes: if the false-decline rate is high, adjust its threshold or add step-up verification instead of blocking, share the data and the rules at a level that does not help fraudsters, and agree a target with the merchant, since its cost of a false decline differs from the average.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c23-10\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C23-10 · Hard · ai · AI</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> A new ranking shows gains in a week-long test. How do you decide whether to launch?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Launch; the test was positive.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Checks guardrails, significance and novelty.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Reads effects by week, plans a longer test and a long-term holdout, handles interference and the creator side, uses a pre-set rule, and states what an offline gain does and does not show.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> A one-week gain is the beginning of a decision, not the end. I'd ask five things.</p>\n<p><em>Is it real?</em> Check validity (sample ratio, logging) and that the result clears the pre-set threshold with an interval, not just a point estimate (C14).</p>\n<p><em>Is it settling?</em> Read the effect by day or week. Suppose the lift is +4.5% in week one, +3.1% in week two, +2.2% in week three and +1.8% in week four: each week is about 70% to 80% of the one before, so the effect is still falling and the week-one figure is mostly novelty. I would wait until it flattens, typically several weeks, before estimating the long-run effect.</p>\n<p><em>What does it cost?</em> Guardrails: retention, satisfaction surveys, reports, latency and cost. A gain in clicks with a fall in retention is a loss.</p>\n<p><em>Does the test measure the whole system?</em> Randomizing users may miss interference: in a feed, one user's content is seen by others; in a marketplace, one buyer's purchase removes supply. A ranking change also changes what creators do over weeks, which a short test cannot see. Where interference is likely, use cluster randomization or measure the other side of the market, and look at creator-side measures.</p>\n<p><em>What is the long-term effect?</em> Launch to most users while keeping a long-term holdout of 1% to 2% for several months to track retention and creator behavior, so the decision can be reversed.</p>\n<p><em>Offline results.</em> An offline improvement in predicting past behavior is a reason to test, not evidence of a product gain, because it reflects the old system's biases and cannot show how people react (the Netflix Prize lesson, CS-C23-02).</p>\n<p><em>Decision.</em> Write the rule first: primary threshold, guardrail margins, minimum duration and what to do in a mixed case. If the rule is met after the effect has settled, launch in stages with the holdout; if not, iterate.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How long should the test run? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Long enough for the effect to stop decaying and to cover a full weekly cycle and, for retention or creator effects, several weeks; practically four to eight weeks for ranking changes, plus a longer holdout after launch. A shorter test is acceptable only for low-risk, reversible changes.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How do you measure the effect on creators? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Track creator-side outcomes in the test: exposure and engagement by creator group, new-creator success, and posting behavior over time. If creators can be randomized or clustered separately from viewers, test that directly; otherwise compare exposure distributions and watch for changes in what is produced.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"c23-12-exercises\"></span>\n<h3>12. Exercises</h3>\n<p>Ten exercises. Each has a model solution and an alternative. Figures are assumptions unless marked, and the arithmetic was computed.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c23-01\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C23-01 · Write a prediction brief</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> Fill in a prediction brief (FW-C23-01) for a model that predicts which deliveries will be late.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> A logistics product wants to warn customers early.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> None; reason.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Six minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> FW-C23-01.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> <em>Decision and actor:</em> warn the customer and, for high-risk deliveries, rebook the route or add capacity; the actors are customers and operations. <em>Label:</em> delivered later than the promised window; produced when the delivery completes, so labels arrive after the prediction (a delay of hours to days) and are clean but late. <em>Data:</em> route, distance, weather, carrier, past lateness by route and time; gaps: new routes and rare events (storms). <em>Costs:</em> a false warning annoys the customer and trains them to ignore warnings; a missed late delivery causes a complaint and a refund. <em>Evaluation:</em> held-out recent weeks, by region and carrier, and calibration (do 70% risk scores mean 70% late?). <em>Monitoring and retraining:</em> weekly, since conditions change; alert when predicted risk and actual lateness diverge.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Start with a rule (any delivery with a delayed pickup is flagged) and compare the model to it.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> Warning too often reduces trust in warnings; set a threshold by the cost of a false warning.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c23-02\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C23-02 · Choose a threshold within capacity</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> A spam classifier on 2,000,000 messages a day (2% spam: 40,000) has two thresholds. Threshold X: recall 90%, precision 60%. Threshold Y: recall 97%, precision 25%. A wrongly flagged good message costs $0.50 and a missed spam costs $4. Reviewers can handle 70,000 flags a day. Compute flagged counts, false positives, false negatives and costs, and choose.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> Threshold choice.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> As above.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Seven minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> FW-C23-02.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> X: true positives 36,000; flagged 36,000 ÷ 0.6 = 60,000; false positives 24,000; false negatives 4,000; cost 24,000 × $0.50 + 4,000 × $4 = $12,000 + $16,000 = $28,000. Y: true positives 38,800; flagged 38,800 ÷ 0.25 = 155,200; false positives 116,400; false negatives 1,200; cost 116,400 × $0.50 + 1,200 × $4 = $58,200 + $4,800 = $63,000. X is cheaper ($28,000 against $63,000) and fits capacity (60,000 ≤ 70,000); Y flags 155,200, more than twice capacity. Choose X. If wrongly flagged messages were automatically filtered with no review, the cost per false positive might be higher; revisit.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Auto-filter the top-scored messages, send a middle band to review, and allow the rest.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> Recall of 97% looks better; its false positives swamp the queue and annoy users.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c23-03\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C23-03 · Base rate and precision</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> A classifier has 80% recall and a 1% false-positive rate. Compute its precision on 1,000,000 items when the base rate is 5%, 1% and 0.1%, and explain what changes.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> Why precision depends on the base rate.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> As above.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Five minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> C23.3 and C12.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> Base 5%: 50,000 bad, 950,000 good; true positives 40,000; false positives 9,500; precision 40,000 ÷ 49,500 = 80.8%. Base 1%: 10,000 bad; true positives 8,000; false positives 9,900; precision 44.7%. Base 0.1%: 1,000 bad; true positives 800; false positives 9,990; precision 7.4%. The classifier is identical in all three; only the proportion of bad items changed. When the target is rare, even a small false-positive rate produces many more false alarms than true catches. A threshold that is sensible at 5% can be far too loose at 0.1%.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Show the same with a 0.1% false-positive rate: at a 0.1% base rate precision would be 800 ÷ (800 + 999) = 44.5%.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> Reducing the false-positive rate is often the highest-value improvement for rare events.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c23-04\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C23-04 · The cost of staging</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> A recommender serves 6,000 requests a second at peak. A ranking model takes 0.15 ms per item. The catalog has 12,000 eligible items per user. Compute the processor cores needed to score all eligible items and to score 600 candidates, and the ratio.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> Why candidate generation exists.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> As above.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Five minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> Worked example 2.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> All items: 12,000 × 6,000 = 72 million scores a second × 0.15 ms = 10,800 processor-seconds a second: about 10,800 cores. Candidates: 600 × 6,000 = 3.6 million scores a second × 0.15 ms = 540 cores. The ratio is 20 to 1. Candidate generation therefore saves 95% of the ranking compute, provided it does not drop good items (its quality is measured by recall).</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Use a lighter model for the first 3,000 and the heavy model on the top 300 (a cascade).</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> Fewer candidates cost less and risk missing good items; measure the recall of the candidate stage.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c23-05\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C23-05 · Compare rankings with NDCG</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> Raters give the top five results grades 0 to 3. Ranking A returns [2, 3, 0, 1, 3]. The ideal ordering of the same results is [3, 3, 2, 1, 0], with DCG 6.32. Compute A's NDCG and say what it means.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> Search quality.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> DCG = sum of grade ÷ log₂(position + 1).</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Six minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> Worked example 4.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> A's DCG = 2 ÷ 1 + 3 ÷ 1.585 + 0 ÷ 2 + 1 ÷ 2.322 + 3 ÷ 2.585 = 2 + 1.893 + 0 + 0.431 + 1.161 = 5.48. NDCG = 5.48 ÷ 6.32 = 0.87. The ranking has all the right results but puts a perfect result (3) at position 5 and a weaker one (2) at position 1; the position discount penalizes that. Precision at 5 (grades 2 and above counted relevant) would show 3 of 5 for both A and the ideal, hiding the difference.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Compare mean NDCG across 300 queries with a standard error to see whether a difference is real.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> NDCG depends on rater guidelines and the choice of how many positions to count.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c23-06\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C23-06 · Correct for position bias</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> In a randomized slice, an item moved from position 1 to position 3 saw its click rate fall from 10% to 4%. Examination of positions suggests the probability that a user looks at position 2 is 0.6 and at position 3 is 0.4 relative to position 1. Item P is shown at position 3 with a 3% click rate; item Q is shown at position 1 with an 8% click rate. Which is better, after correcting for position?</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> Learning from clicks.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> As above.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Five minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> C23.6.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> Correct each click rate by dividing by the relative chance of being looked at: P: 3% ÷ 0.4 = 7.5% equivalent at position 1; Q: 8% ÷ 1 = 8%. Q is slightly better than P (8% against 7.5%), a much smaller gap than the raw 3% against 8%. The raw numbers would have led a model to rank P far below Q. The randomized slice gave the factor (4% ÷ 10% = 0.4 for position 3), consistent with the examination estimate. Correcting for position stops the model from learning that top positions are good.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Randomize positions for a small share of traffic and compare items at the same position.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> Randomization costs a little engagement; it gives unbiased estimates.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c23-07\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C23-07 · Read a ranking experiment</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> A new recommendation ranking has: plays +2.4% (interval +1.9% to +2.9%); 28-day retention +0.1 points (−0.3 to +0.5), margin −0.3; diversity of titles watched −6% (−8% to −4%), margin −3%; weekly plays lift +3.5%, +2.8%, +2.2%, +1.9%. Decide.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> A launch decision.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> As above.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Six minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> FW-C23-05 and FW-C17-04.</p>\n";
